<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khas people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khas are an Indo-Aryan people of the western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, the original speakers of the language that became Nepali, once called Khas kura. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasa-malla-kingdom">Khasa Malla kingdom</a> ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinja">Sinja</a> near Jumla, and Khas soldiers later formed much of the army of the Gorkha kingdom. Most Khas belong to the Chhetri and related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of Nepal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khas-people.jpg" alt="Khas people" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khaptad Digital Music · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deuda_dancers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
